\section{Grouped families}{


Four families read a grouping of the candidate columns. They are fitted by
the penalised optimiser (\code{workflow = "penalized_optimizer"}) and have no
brms form, so asking for one on the Bayesian route refuses by name rather
than substituting the nearest prior.
\itemize{
\item \code{group_lasso(scale, group, alpha)}, with \code{scale} > 0 and \code{alpha} in
(0, 1]: a group enters or leaves as a whole.
\item \code{sparse_group_lasso(scale, alpha, group)}, with \code{alpha} in (0, 1): a
group enters as a whole, and thins inside.
\item \code{exclusive_lasso(scale, group)}, with \code{scale} > 0: members of a group
compete.
\item \code{group_exclusive(group_scale, exclusive_scale, group)}, with both
scales > 0: both at once.
}

\code{group} declares how the columns are grouped, and it is never inferred
from the data. \code{"term"} makes each model term one group, so a factor's
non-reference contrast columns move together and a numeric main effect is
a group of one. Under ordinary treatment contrasts the reference level has
no design column and is therefore not inserted into the penalised group;
its meaning remains in the intercept and the other protected terms.
\code{"block"} makes the specification's own target set one group, which
is how a cluster of correlated measurements is declared to be a set of
competitors. \code{group_lasso()}, \code{sparse_group_lasso()} and
\code{group_exclusive()} default to \code{"term"}; \code{exclusive_lasso()} defaults to
\code{"block"}. The rule and the resolved groups are recorded on the
certificate. A grouping in which every group has one column refuses, since
a group lasso would then be a lasso and an exclusive lasso a ridge.

\code{group_lasso()} charges \verb{(alpha / scale) * sum_g sqrt(p_g) ||beta_g||_2},
where \code{p_g} is the number of columns in group \code{g}. With \code{alpha} below one
(default 1) it is a group elastic net and also charges
\verb{((1 - alpha) / (2 * scale)) * ||beta||_2^2}; a group still enters or
leaves as a whole.

The exclusive penalty adds \verb{sum_g (sum_j |beta_j|)^2 / 2} over groups,
which encourages competition inside a group and suits a declared set of
correlated alternatives, for example one soil property measured at several
depths. It does not guarantee that exactly one member is non-zero, and on a
group of one it is exactly \code{ridge()} at the same scale.

\code{adaptive()} remains withdrawn and refuses by name. An adaptive penalty
needs a first-stage estimate and a rule for turning that estimate into
weights, and this release ships neither.
